# ==============================================================================
# .env.example - committed template for DigitalVault.
#
# Contains PLACEHOLDERS ONLY. Never put a real secret in this file.
# Copy it to .env and fill in the values:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# This file is fully annotated; read the comments before filling anything in.
# It is force-included past the .env* ignore rule on purpose, because a
# template with no template cannot be configured without guessing.
# ==============================================================================
################################################################################
# DigitalVault — ENVIRONMENT TEMPLATE
################################################################################
# ALL VALUES BELOW ARE PLACEHOLDERS. Nothing here is a real secret.
# Replace every REPLACE_ME / placeholder with a real value before deploying.
#
# This file is gitignored (.env*) and must NEVER be committed.
# The committed, secret-free twin of this file is .env.example.
#
# WHERE TO PUT VALUES
#   - Local dev ....... this file, in the project root. Next.js + Bun both read
#                      it automatically (Bun auto-loads .env from the cwd).
#   - Vercel .......... do NOT upload this file. Set each variable in
#                      Vercel -> Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables,
#                      for BOTH "Production" and "Preview".
#   - Render .......... do NOT upload this file either. Set each variable in
#                      Render -> your Web Service -> Environment, then
#                      "Save changes" (saving triggers a redeploy).
#
# QUICK START (minimum to boot in production)
#   1. DATABASE_URL        <- Supabase -> Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string
#   2. SESSION_SECRET      <- openssl rand -base64 48   (MUST be >= 32 chars)
#   3. NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL <- https://your-app.onrender.com  (no trailing slash)
#
# The site DEPLOYS AND BOOTS with none of these set: every public page
# renders with built-in default content and only database-backed API
# endpoints return 500, so you can go live FIRST and add variables one
# at a time, redeploying after each one.
# DIRECT_URL is only needed when YOU run Prisma schema commands yourself
# (`prisma migrate deploy` / `prisma db push`); the schema no longer
# declares `directUrl`, so the running app never reads it.
#
# Every variable below was verified against the source. Anything not listed here
# is either managed by the platform or read by the app from the database.
################################################################################


################################################################################
# 1. DATABASE  ***  SECRET  ***
################################################################################
# Supabase (PostgreSQL) pooled connection used by the RUNNING application.
#
# Where to find it:
#   Supabase dashboard -> your project -> Settings -> Database -> Connection string
#   Choose the "Session pooler" (URI) tab. It looks like:
#
#     postgresql://postgres.PROJECTREF:YOURPASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# Two query parameters are REQUIRED for serverless, and are already in the
# placeholders below:
#
#   pgbouncer=true      route through Supabase's transaction pooler. Supabase
#                       closes idle serverless connections aggressively, so
#                       unpooled connections drop under load.
#   connection_limit=1  each Vercel serverless instance opens its OWN pool.
#                       Without this cap, a handful of instances exhaust
#                       Supabase's connection budget and the site starts
#                       throwing "too many connections".
#
# SECRET: this string embeds your database password.
#
# LOCAL DEV: the schema is PostgreSQL everywhere (see prisma/schema.prisma),
# so local development needs a Postgres too — either a local instance or
# the same Supabase URI. The old SQLite file (prisma/dev.db) is NOT usable
# anymore: a `file:` URL is rejected by the postgresql provider.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.REPLACE_PROJECT_REF:REPLACE_DB_PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"


################################################################################
# 2. DIRECT_URL  ***  SECRET  ***
################################################################################
# Supabase DIRECT (non-pooled) connection. Used only when YOU run Prisma
# schema commands (`prisma migrate deploy`, `prisma db push`, Prisma
# Studio, seed scripts) — anything that talks to the schema rather than
# to data. The app itself never reads this variable (the schema has no
# `directUrl`), so it is OPTIONAL for deploys.
#
# Where to find it:
#   Same Supabase page as DATABASE_URL, but the "Direct connection" tab
#   (NOT the pooler). It looks like:
#
#     postgresql://postgres.PROJECTREF:YOURPASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.supabase.com:5432/postgres
#
# RECOMMENDED (not required) when running schema commands yourself:
# schema pushes through the Session pooler can hang under load, so
# bypass it with the direct connection for those commands only.
#
# SECRET: embeds your database password.
#
# NOTE: this variable is read by Prisma via env(), so a plain-text search for
# "process.env" in the source will NOT find it. It is easy to forget — and
# forgetting it is the single most common cause of a failed first deploy.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.REPLACE_PROJECT_REF:REPLACE_DB_PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.supabase.com:5432/postgres"


################################################################################
# 3. SESSION_SECRET  ***  SECRET  ***  REQUIRED
################################################################################
# The HMAC key that signs every session cookie.
#
# Anyone who learns this value can mint a valid cookie for ANY user, including
# an admin. Treat a leak as a full compromise and rotate immediately.
#
# GENERATE A FRESH, UNIQUE ONE (do not copy the placeholder, do not reuse the
# local dev value):
#
#     openssl rand -base64 48
#
# or, if you have no openssl:
#
#     bun -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID()+crypto.randomUUID()+crypto.randomUUID())"
#
# RULES ENFORCED BY THE APP (src/lib/password.ts):
#   - must be at least 32 characters
#   - must not be one of the placeholders that have shipped in this repo
#   - the guard throws LAZILY — only when a session is signed or verified
#     (i.e. on login / auth API calls). The site still boots and serves
#     every public page without SESSION_SECRET; only the login flow 500s.
#     If auth endpoints 500, check this variable first.
# WHY THE PLACEHOLDER BELOW IS DELIBERATELY SHORT (11 chars, under the
# 32-char minimum): so that if you deploy without replacing it, every
# login attempt fails LOUDLY instead of quietly signing session cookies
# with a value published in a git repo. A long, realistic-looking
# placeholder would sail past that guard and hand every reader admin
# access. Public pages still work; replace it before turning auth on.
SESSION_SECRET="REPLACE_ME"


################################################################################
# 4. NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL  ***  PUBLIC — not a secret ***
################################################################################
# The real, public origin of this deployment. No trailing slash.
#
#     https://your-app.onrender.com
#
# IMPORTANT — this value is INLINED INTO THE BROWSER BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME.
# Changing it later requires a full REDEPLOY on your host; restarting the
# server is NOT enough. This is the single most common deploy gotcha in
# this project.
#
# Used for:
#   - Google OAuth redirect_uri
#   - <link rel="canonical"> and OpenGraph URLs
#   - sitemap.xml and robots.txt
#   - admin-generated set-password / password-reset links in emails
#
# This is the variable whose absence caused the bug where a deployed site
# redirected visitors to http://localhost:3000.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_DOMAIN"


################################################################################
# 5. SITE_URL  (optional, not a secret)
################################################################################
# Server-only canonical-origin override. Takes priority over
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL when resolving links generated on the server.
#
# You normally do NOT need this — NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL already covers the
# deployed site, and platform-injected hosts (VERCEL_URL on Vercel,
# RENDER_EXTERNAL_URL on Render, DEPLOY_PRIME_URL on Netlify) act as
# automatic fallbacks. Set it only if server-generated links must differ
# from the public origin (e.g. an internal canonical host).
#
# Not visible to the browser: only NEXT_PUBLIC_* survives into the client
# bundle, so this reads as undefined in client components.
#SITE_URL="https://REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_DOMAIN"


################################################################################
# 6. GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID  (not a secret)
################################################################################
# OAuth client ID for "Continue with Google" sign-in.
#
# Where to find it:
#   Google Cloud Console -> project -> APIs & Services -> Credentials
#   -> OAuth 2.0 Client IDs -> (your Web application client) -> Client ID
#
# Also required — the Authorized redirect URI in the same Google screen must be
# EXACTLY this, character for character (scheme, host and path all compared):
#
#     https://your-app.vercel.app/api/auth/google/callback
#
# Replace "your-app.vercel.app" with your real NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL host.
# Without a matching entry Google rejects the callback with redirect_uri_mismatch.
#
# If unset, the app shows a friendly error and no OAuth flow starts — the rest
# of the site keeps working, so this is safe to leave blank until you need it.
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID.apps.googleusercontent.com"


################################################################################
# 7. GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET  ***  SECRET  ***
################################################################################
# The client secret for the OAuth client above. Same Credentials screen.
# Google shows it only once at creation — if lost, create a new client.
# Never commit it.
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET"


################################################################################
# 8. GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION  (not a secret)
################################################################################
# The google-site-verification meta token, used ONLY to prove you own the
# domain to Google Search Console. Rendered as a <meta> tag in the document
# head by src/app/layout.tsx. Plain text, no prefix.
#
# Where to find it: Search Console -> your property -> Settings -> Ownership
# verification -> HTML tag -> copy the "content=" value.
#
# Setting this does NOT enable Search Console itself; the site must also be
# indexed (it needs a valid sitemap.xml, which requires NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL).
#GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION="REPLACE_WITH_abc123def456_content_value"


################################################################################
# 9. CRON_SECRET  ***  SECRET  ***
################################################################################
# Bearer token for POST /api/admin/chat/autoclose — the endpoint that
# auto-closes stale support conversations.
#
# That route accepts EITHER an authenticated admin session OR this bearer
# token. The token exists so a scheduled job (Vercel Cron) can call it without
# a browser session.
#
# Any long random string will do; reuse your SESSION_SECRET if you prefer to
# keep the variable count down, but a distinct value is better practice:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#CRON_SECRET="REPLACE_WITH_A_LONG_RANDOM_STRING"


################################################################################
# 10. API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN  (optional, not a secret)
################################################################################
# Global backstop ceiling for API requests per minute per IP address.
#
# Leave unset for the default, which is 300/min in production and unlimited
# in development. Set it only to tighten or relax that global ceiling; the
# fine-grained per-route limiters (login, register, forgot-password) are NOT
# affected by this and are hard-coded.
#
# The default is deliberately high: it exists to stop floods, not to interfere
# with the chat widget's 1-second polling.
#
# Known limitation: counters are in-memory, so they reset on every cold start
# and are per-instance. Fine for a small site; move to Redis/KV if you run
# several instances.
#API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN="300"


################################################################################
# 11. ANALYZE  (build-time only, not a secret)
################################################################################
# Set to "true" ONLY for a one-off bundle-analysis build — it makes
# next.config.ts write a bundle report you can inspect. Leave unset normally;
# it has no effect on a normal production build.
#ANALYZE="true"


################################################################################
# 12. SEED PASSWORDS  (LOCAL ONLY — not needed on Vercel)
################################################################################
# Consumed by scripts/seed.ts, which creates the demo admin and buyer accounts.
#
# If a variable is unset the seeder MINTS A RANDOM PASSWORD and prints it
# exactly once — it never falls back to a known default. Set these only to
# choose your own local dev passwords:
#
#     set ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD to some value of at least 8 characters
#     set BUYER_SEED_PASSWORD to some value of at least 8 characters
#
# Do NOT set these in the Vercel dashboard. The seeder refuses to run when
# NODE_ENV=production, so it cannot seed a live database by accident.
#
#SECURITY NOTE: the previous demo credentials (admin / demo account passwords)
#were removed from the codebase during the security remediation. If you are
#still using the old defaults on the original SQLite database, rotate them:
#     bun scripts/set-passwords.mjs
#ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD="REPLACE_WITH_A_LOCAL_ONLY_ADMIN_PASSWORD"
#BUYER_SEED_PASSWORD="REPLACE_WITH_A_LOCAL_ONLY_BUYER_PASSWORD"


################################################################################
# DO NOT SET — managed by the platform
################################################################################
# NODE_ENV    Vercel sets this to "production" automatically. Setting it by
#              hand locally is how people accidentally trigger production-only
#              code paths.
# VERCEL_URL  Vercel sets this per deployment (e.g. "my-app-abc123.vercel.app").
#              The app uses it purely as a safety net: if NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL
#              is ever missing, generated links degrade to the real deployment
#              host instead of http://localhost:3000.
# DEV_ORIGIN  Not an env var — a constant in src/lib/site-url.ts used only as
#              the last-resort developer-laptop fallback.


################################################################################
# NOT ENV VARS — configured elsewhere
################################################################################
# GOOGLE TAG MANAGER / GA4 / META PIXEL
#   There is deliberately no GTM_ID here. In this app, analytics snippets are
#   stored in the DATABASE and pasted through the admin panel at
#   /admin -> Settings -> Tracking, so you can change them without a redeploy.
#   The code that renders them is src/lib/tracking.ts and
#   src/app/api/admin/tracking/route.ts.
#   googletagmanager.com and google-analytics.com are ALREADY whitelisted in
#   the Content-Security-Policy in src/proxy.ts, so a standard GTM snippet
#   works without any further CSP change.
#   IMPORTANT: anyone with admin access can paste arbitrary JavaScript that
#   runs on every public page. That is inherent to paste-your-own-snippet
#   analytics, so the admin account is the security boundary — protect its
#   password and revoke sessions on staff offboarding.
#   The tracking UI also refuses obviously hostile payloads (cross-origin
#   iframes, document.cookie exfiltration, fetch to unvetted origins,
#   event-handler attributes) at save time.
#
# SITE-WIDE SETTINGS (deposit addresses, crypto wallets, coupons, banners, …)
#   Also database-backed, editable from the admin panel — not env vars.
#
# FILE UPLOADS
#   Stored in public/uploads/ on the local filesystem. On Vercel that
#   filesystem is read-only and ephemeral, so uploaded files will NOT survive
#   a redeploy. Switch uploads to Supabase Storage or an external bucket
#   before going live. (No product in the seed data currently references an
#   uploaded image, so nothing breaks in the meantime.)


################################################################################
# CHECKLIST BEFORE DEPLOYING
################################################################################
# [ ] DATABASE_URL set — pooled, with pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
# [ ] DIRECT_URL set — non-pooled
# [ ] SESSION_SECRET set — freshly generated, 32+ chars, not a placeholder
# [ ] NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL set to the real origin, then the project REDEPLOYED
# [ ] Google OAuth client created + Authorized redirect URI registered
# [ ] Schema pushed:  bun run db:push   (Supabase DB is empty otherwise)
# [ ] Seed data loaded, or accounts created via the admin panel
# [ ] Upload storage moved off the Vercel filesystem (see above)
# [ ] This file NOT committed  (.env* is gitignored — keep it that way)
################################################################################